            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#mistakes"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >7. Mistakes First-Time Buyers Make</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#which-guide"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >8. Which of Our Guides Should You Read Next?</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >9. Frequently Asked Questions</a
            >
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p
          class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
        >
          Most people start shopping for a treadmill by comparing model names
          and star ratings. That's backwards. The models that end up right
          for you depend entirely on decisions you can make before you ever
          open a product page — how you'll actually use it, how much floor
          space you have, and what you're willing to spend. This guide walks
          through those decisions first, then points you to the specific
          model roundup that matches what you land on.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="use-case" class="scroll-mt-24">Start With Your Use Case</h2>
        <p>
          Before you look at a single spec, answer one question honestly: how
          fast will you actually be moving on this thing, most of the time?
          Treadmill shoppers consistently overestimate their typical pace,
          picturing themselves training for a 10K when the realistic daily
          use case is a brisk walk while catching up on a podcast. That's not
          a knock — walking is genuinely excellent exercise — but it changes
          which specs matter.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're walking most sessions, at speeds up to about 4 mph, you
          can comfortably shop in a lower horsepower and smaller deck range
          and save real money. If you're jogging or running regularly, at 5
          mph and above, you need a stronger motor, a longer deck, and better
          cushioning, or you'll outgrow a walking-focused machine within
          months and feel it working harder than it should. If your use case
          is mixed — some walking meetings during the workday, some real
          runs on the weekend — size up rather than down, since a machine
          built for running handles walking effortlessly, but the reverse
          isn't true.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also think about who else in the household will use it. A
          treadmill bought for one runner's needs might be undersized for a
          taller partner or a teenager who also wants to use it, and weight
          capacity in particular needs to comfortably clear the heaviest
          likely user with room to spare, not just the person doing the
          shopping.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <h2 id="motor-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Motor Power (CHP), Explained
        </h2>
        <p>
          Every treadmill spec sheet lists a horsepower number, and it's
          nearly always the most misleading figure on the page. Manufacturers
          love to advertise "peak" horsepower, a number the motor can
          theoretically hit for a few seconds under ideal conditions and will
          never actually sustain during a real workout. The number that
          matters is <strong>continuous horsepower (CHP)</strong> — the power
          output the motor can maintain reliably over a full session without
          overheating or straining.
        </p>
        <p>
          As a rough guide: 2.0-2.5 CHP is enough for walking, 2.5-3.0 CHP is
          a reasonable minimum if you jog regularly, and 3.0 CHP or higher is
          worth prioritizing if you run consistently, use interval training,
          or have more than one regular user with a heavier body weight. A
          motor pushed consistently beyond its comfortable range tends to run
          hot, get louder over time, and wear out faster — a pattern we've
          seen repeatedly in long-term testing of underpowered machines used
          for a purpose they weren't built for.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a listing only shows a "peak" horsepower figure with no
          continuous rating disclosed anywhere, treat that as a mild red flag
          about how transparent the rest of the spec sheet is likely to be.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                2.0-2.5
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                CHP for Walking
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                2.5-3.0
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                CHP Minimum for Jogging
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                3.0+
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                CHP for Running/Multi-User
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                20-30
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                Lbs. Capacity Buffer to Leave
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <h2 id="deck-size" class="scroll-mt-24">Deck Size and Cushioning</h2>
        <p>
          Deck size is measured as width by length, and both numbers matter
          more than shoppers usually expect. A narrow deck, anything under
          about 18 inches wide, can feel genuinely uncomfortable at faster
          paces, since your natural stride has less side-to-side margin for
          error. A short deck, anything under about 50 inches for anyone over
          roughly 5'8", can make running feel cramped, with your stride
          landing uncomfortably close to the front or back of the belt.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cushioning is the other half of the equation and is much harder to
          judge from a spec sheet alone. Manufacturers use varying terms —
          "variable response cushioning," "flex deck," "shock absorption
          zones" — that all point at the same basic idea: a system designed
          to soften impact at your heel strike while staying firmer where you
          push off. In our testing, higher-end cushioning systems make a
          noticeable difference for anyone with knee or joint sensitivity,
          while flatter, firmer decks on entry-level machines are perfectly
          fine for walkers but start to feel harsh during longer or faster
          running sessions.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you can test a treadmill in person before buying, actually jog
          on it for a minute rather than just walking. Cushioning
          differences that are barely noticeable at a walking pace become
          much more obvious once you're landing with real impact.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="folding-vs-fixed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Folding vs. Fixed-Frame
        </h2>
        <p>
          This is often the single biggest decision in choosing a home
          treadmill, and it comes down almost entirely to your available
          space rather than performance. A fixed-frame treadmill is
          generally sturdier per dollar spent, since the frame doesn't need
          to accommodate a fold hinge, and there's simply one less moving
          mechanical part that can eventually wear or loosen. If you have a
          dedicated home gym, a garage, or a room where the treadmill can
          live permanently, a fixed frame is usually the better value.
        </p>
        <p>
          A folding treadmill trades a small amount of that structural
          simplicity for the ability to reclaim your floor space. For
          apartments, shared living rooms, home offices that double as guest
          rooms, or renters who expect to move within a year or two, that
          trade-off is well worth it. The fold mechanism itself matters too —
          hydraulic-assist folds are dramatically easier to live with day to
          day than manual latch systems if you'll be folding the machine
          more than once or twice a week. We cover this distinction in much
          more depth, including specific tested models, in our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          One nuance worth flagging: folding treadmills, especially
          ultra-compact ones, often achieve their small folded footprint
          partly through a shorter deck and a lighter frame, which can mean
          a lower weight capacity and a less comfortable running experience
          than an equivalently priced fixed-frame machine. If you're tall,
          run rather than walk, or want to maximize deck comfort, it's worth
          comparing a mid-range folding model against an entry-level fixed
          model at a similar price before deciding.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <h2 id="connectivity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Incline, Consoles, and Subscriptions
        </h2>
        <p>
          Automatic incline is one of the more genuinely useful features on
          modern treadmills, since it lets you add resistance and calorie
          burn without increasing speed or impact — a real benefit if you
          have joint concerns. Most home treadmills top out somewhere between
          10% and 15% incline, which is plenty for the large majority of home
          users; decline, offered on a smaller number of premium machines, is
          more of a nice-to-have than a necessity for most people.
        </p>
        <p>
          Consoles range from bare-bones LED displays showing speed, time,
          and distance, up through large touchscreens running full coaching
          platforms like iFit or JRNY. The honest question to ask yourself
          is whether you'll actually use guided, trainer-led workouts, or
          whether you mostly want to set a pace and run your own session. If
          it's the latter, paying extra for touchscreen hardware and a
          required monthly subscription is money that buys you nothing
          you'll use. If structured coaching genuinely helps you stay
          consistent, the subscription cost — typically somewhere around
          $20-$40 a month — is worth budgeting for as an ongoing cost, not a
          one-time purchase.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bluetooth speaker support and heart rate monitor pairing are
          common even on mid-range machines now and are worth having, but
          rarely worth paying a significant premium for on their own if a
          cheaper model otherwise meets your needs.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <h2 id="budget-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          What You Get at Each Budget Tier
        </h2>
        <p>
          Treadmill pricing follows a fairly predictable pattern, and knowing
          roughly what to expect at each tier helps you avoid both
          overpaying for features you won't use and underbuying a machine
          that can't keep up with how you actually plan to train.
        </p>

        <div
          class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200"
        >
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
                <th
                  class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200"
                >
                  Price Tier
                </th>
                <th
                  class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200"
                >
                  Typical Motor
                </th>
                <th
                  class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200"
                >
                  Typical Deck
                </th>
                <th
                  class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200"
                >
                  Best For
                </th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                BUDGET_TIERS.map((row, i) => (
                  <tr class={i < BUDGET_TIERS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                    <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900">{row.tier}</td>
                    <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.motor}</td>
                    <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.deck}</td>
                    <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.bestFor}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Most households land comfortably in the $600-$1,200 range for a
          machine that handles daily walking and moderate jogging without
          feeling flimsy. If your budget caps out under $500, that's not a
          dealbreaker — it just means being realistic that the machine is
          best suited to walking rather than serious running. We break down
          specific tested picks at the budget end of the market in our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews"
            >treadmills under $1,000 guide</a
          >.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <h2 id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Mistakes First-Time Buyers Make
        </h2>
        <p>
          A few patterns show up again and again in the buyer feedback and
          owner reviews we look through while researching our guides. Here
          are the ones worth avoiding.
        </p>
        <h3>Not measuring the space first</h3>
        <p>
          This is, by a wide margin, the most common mistake. "Compact" and
          "space-saving" are relative marketing terms, not measurements.
          Measure the exact spot the treadmill will live — including ceiling
          height if you're considering a folding model that stands upright —
          before you fall in love with a specific machine online.
        </p>
        <h3>Shopping on peak horsepower instead of continuous horsepower</h3>
        <p>
          As covered above, peak horsepower numbers are inflated and largely
          meaningless for real-world use. If a listing doesn't clearly state
          a continuous horsepower rating, dig for it in the manual or
          spec sheet before assuming the machine can handle your intended
          pace.
        </p>
        <h3>Buying right at the weight capacity limit</h3>
        <p>
          A stated weight capacity is the absolute upper bound under ideal
          conditions, not a comfortable operating range. Leave a buffer of at
          least 20-30 pounds below the stated limit for the heaviest regular
          user, since running impact adds meaningful dynamic load beyond
          simple standing body weight.
        </p>
        <h3>Ignoring the ongoing cost of a subscription-dependent console</h3>
        <p>
          A touchscreen console that requires a monthly subscription to
          unlock most of its value effectively has a hidden recurring cost
          on top of the sticker price. Factor a year or two of that
          subscription into your real budget comparison against a simpler,
          subscription-free machine.
        </p>
        <h3>Skipping the return policy and assembly details</h3>
        <p>
          Treadmills are heavy, and full assembly is often more involved than
          buyers expect — sometimes requiring two people. Check the seller's
          return and exchange policy before buying, since returning a
          200-pound machine that turns out to be the wrong fit is a far
          bigger hassle than double-checking dimensions and specs up front.
        </p>

        <div
          class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8"
        >
          <p class="font-black text-red-900 mb-3">
            Quick Pre-Purchase Checklist
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-800 text-sm">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-black">&bull;</span>
              Measured the exact space, including ceiling height for folding
              models.
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-black">&bull;</span>
              Confirmed continuous horsepower (CHP), not just peak horsepower.
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-black">&bull;</span>
              Checked weight capacity against the heaviest regular user, with
              a buffer.
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-black">&bull;</span>
              Decided honestly whether you'll use a subscription coaching
              app.
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="text-red-500 font-black">&bull;</span>
              Reviewed the return policy before assembly begins.
            </li>
