        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What is the best treadmill for a small home?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For a small home or apartment, a folding treadmill with a
              hydraulic-assist fold mechanism is usually the best fit, since
              it lets you reclaim the floor space when the machine isn't in
              use. Look for a folded footprint under roughly <strong
                >34 x 34 inches</strong
              > and confirm the deck is still at least 18 x 50 inches if you plan
              to jog rather than only walk.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How much should I spend on a home treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Most households land in the <strong>$600-$1,200</strong> range for
              a treadmill that comfortably handles daily walking and moderate
              jogging without feeling flimsy. Spending under $500 is fine for
              occasional walking use, while serious runners or multi-person
              households often see real durability benefits from crossing
              into the $1,200-$2,000 range.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What horsepower do I actually need?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Look at continuous horsepower (CHP), not peak horsepower, since
              peak numbers are inflated marketing figures. <strong
                >2.0-2.5 CHP</strong
              > comfortably handles walking, <strong>2.5-3.0 CHP</strong> is a
              safe minimum for regular jogging, and <strong>3.0+ CHP</strong> is
              worth prioritizing if you run regularly or if more than one person
              in your household will use the machine.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is it worth paying extra for a treadmill with a subscription
              app?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on whether you'll actually use guided workouts.
              Platforms like iFit and JRNY genuinely help some people stay
              consistent by auto-adjusting incline and pace during
              trainer-led sessions, but the monthly subscription cost adds up
              quickly and the hardware is often priced assuming you'll
              subscribe. If you prefer running your own pace without
              coaching, a simpler console will save you money without
              costing you anything you'd actually use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Should I buy a folding or a fixed-frame treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Choose folding if floor space is limited or you rent and may
              need to relocate the machine easily. Choose a fixed-frame
              treadmill if you have a dedicated home gym area, since fixed
              frames are generally sturdier per dollar and skip the added
              mechanical complexity of a fold hinge entirely.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What's the biggest mistake first-time treadmill buyers make?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Buying based on peak horsepower and a long features list
              instead of measuring their available space first. A treadmill
              that doesn't physically fit where it needs to live, or that
              requires a fold mechanism the buyer didn't realize they'd need,
              ends up unused far more often than one bought with a smaller
              feature set but a clear-eyed view of the room it has to live
              in.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
